WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.940
Chapter six. The plumbing,

2
00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:03.860
and the thing people get wrong about it.

3
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.580
Two ideas here, and then one warning that

4
00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:07.760
matters more than both of them.

5
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.920
First idea. Model Context Protocol,

6
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.720
usually shortened to MCP.

7
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.860
Anthropic published it in late 2024 and gave

8
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.460
it away. The problem it solves is unglamorous

9
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:20.100
and enormous. Before it,

10
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.360
every model needed a custom integration to

11
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.060
every tool. Three models and three tools meant

12
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.080
nine separate builds.

13
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:27.260
Add a fourth model,

14
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.760
build three more. With one standard,

15
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.300
you write a tool once and any model can use

16
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.820
it. The analogy everyone reaches for is a

17
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.960
charging socket, and for once the analogy

18
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.420
holds up. Second idea.

19
00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:41.980
A connector is one of those sockets with the

20
00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:42.860
wiring already done.

21
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.940
Gmail, Drive, Notion,

22
00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.320
Slack, GitHub. You sign in once through the

23
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.200
normal login screen and tick what it is allowed

24
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.120
to touch. Grant it the narrowest permission

25
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.260
that still does the job,

26
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.860
and revoke it when the job is finished.

27
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.800
Now the warning. There is a difference between

28
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.400
running something locally and running it in

29
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.580
the cloud, and almost everybody gets this

30
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:06.420
backwards. Local means the hands are on your

31
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.560
machine. It can read your actual files,

32
00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.360
run real commands, reach things only your

33
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.920
laptop can reach. What local does not mean

34
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.120
is private. The reasoning still happens on

35
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.400
servers belonging to somebody else.

36
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.800
Every file it reads locally gets sent off

37
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.840
to be processed, exactly like anything you

38
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:24.580
paste into a browser.

39
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.860
So the rule is the boring one,

40
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.340
and it has not changed.

41
00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:29.660
Nothing classified,

42
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:31.980
nothing marked, no work documents,

43
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.000
and no personal data belonging to other people.

44
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.040
That holds on every tier,

45
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:38.660
including the local one.

46
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:41.960
If you would not email it to a vendor,

47
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.120
do not prompt it.

